PART III – SECURITY, VALIDATION & OPERATIONS
31. Frontend Security Requirements
•	Never store passwords.
•	Never embed application secrets in the SPA.
•	Mask account/card identifiers by default.
•	Never trust account IDs, role claims or balances submitted by the browser without server validation.
•	Do not determine authorization, transfer limits, risk approval or final transaction result in React.
•	Do not log access tokens, refresh tokens, credentials or full account numbers.
•	Do not expose database IDs where a safer public identifier is available.
•	Sanitize/escape user-controlled content; do not inject arbitrary HTML.
•	Require HTTPS in all non-local environments.
•	Use explicit confirmation for destructive or high-risk actions.
32. Browser and Edge Security Controls
Control	Requirement
CSP	Restrict script, frame, image, connection and style origins; remove unsafe allowances where possible.
HSTS	Enforce HTTPS after secure deployment is established.
X-Content-Type-Options	nosniff
Referrer-Policy	Use a restrictive policy appropriate for the deployment.
Permissions-Policy	Disable browser capabilities not required by the application.
CORS	Allow only approved frontend origins for authenticated banking APIs.
TLS	HTTPS only; modern protocol/cipher policy managed at edge/load balancer.

33. Authorization Model
The UI may hide or disable actions based on roles/claims such as CUSTOMER, SUPPORT or ADMIN, but this is only a presentation convenience. Every backend endpoint must independently enforce authorization and customer/resource ownership.
34. Accessibility
•	Target WCAG 2.2 AA.
•	Full keyboard navigation and visible focus states.
•	Explicit form labels and programmatic association of error messages with fields.
•	Screen-reader announcement for transfer status changes.
•	Color is never the only indication of success, pending or failure.
•	Mobile touch targets should be large enough for reliable use.
•	Logical heading hierarchy and semantic tables.
•	Support browser zoom and reflow.
